course: dictionary
lesson: 1563
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. The word chap is a good example. Its meaning depends on the words around it.
explain: «Look here, Mole old chap, you seem dead tired.» Old chap is a friendly way of talking to a man or boy. The Rat is not talking about a leather coat or a cracked lip here. Ask yourself: does this meaning fit the sentence?
explain: «Cold wind and dry heating take water from the skin; licking your lips makes them more chapped.» Here chapped is a verb describing skin. The clues are cold wind, dry heating and lip balm, so chapped means dry and cracked. When a word has several meanings, try each one in the sentence and keep the one that makes sense.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: One word can have several meanings, and context tells you which one is right.
fact: A chap is a man or boy. This is informal and a bit old-fashioned, used mostly in Britain.
fact: Chaps are leather leg coverings that cowboys wear over their trousers.
fact: To chap means for skin or lips to become dry, rough and cracked, usually from cold or wind. In Scotland and Northern Ireland, to chap also means to knock on a door.
exercise: «Bustle about, old chap!» What does chap mean in this sentence? | A leather leg covering | To knock on a door | *A man or boy, used in a friendly way | A dry, cracked lip
exercise: In a western film, the cowboy pulls on his chaps before riding out. What are chaps? | Dry, cracked lips | A knock at the door | *Leather coverings worn over trousers | Small wooden boxes
exercise: Lip balm helps «chapped lips» after a cold day. What does chapped mean? | Knocked loudly on | Warm and rosy | Covered in mud | *Dry, rough and cracked
test: In Scotland, Ella hears, «Chap the door, the bus is here.» What does chap mean? | *To knock on a door | Leather leg coverings | A man or boy | To go dry and cracked
test: The Rat says, «I'm the chap that keeps company with gaol-birds.» A gaol-bird is someone who is often in prison. What is the Rat saying about himself? | He has chapped lips from the cold | He is a cowboy in leather chaps | He is knocking on a door | *He is a man who spends time with people who are often in prison
exam: The Rat says, «Mole old chap, you seem dead tired.» Explain in your own words what chap means here, and say which clue in the sentence helped you decide. | *A friendly word for a man or boy, and the clue is old chap, a friendly name | To knock on a door | Dry, cracked skin | A leather covering for the legs
